12 t class excavator model

The meshes (*.obj), the texture (zx120.png) and the kinematic chain in excavator.xml are derived
from the zx120_description package in the zx120_ros repository by the Public Works Research
Institute (PWRI), Japan:

    https://github.com/pwri-opera/zx120_ros
    commit 952ce99bf86991a6d3cfd351b12e1ab2686850fd

That work is licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0; a copy is in the file LICENSE next
to this notice. The source describes a Hitachi ZX120 hydraulic excavator. Hitachi and ZX120 are
trademarks of their owners; this model is not endorsed by them.

Changes made to the source:

- Format conversion: the COLLADA (.dae) meshes for the track, body, boom, arm and bucket links
  were converted to OBJ with their normals and texture coordinates, with each link's URDF
  visual origin applied. The 4096 x 4096 texture (zx120.png) was reduced to 1024 x 1024 by
  averaging 4 x 4 pixel blocks. Specular and shininess values for the paint, and a plain steel
  material for the cylinder rods, are added.
- The boom, arm and bucket cylinder meshes (zx120-*-cyl1.dae barrels, zx120-*-cyl2.dae rods)
  and the bucket linkage meshes (zx120-p1.dae side link, zx120-p2.dae H-link) were converted to
  OBJ and turned so the cylinder axis, or the line between a link's two pin holes, lies along
  an axis. Each barrel was moved so its base pin sits at the origin, each rod so the far end of
  the rod does, and each link so one pin hole does. They are drawn only, posed from the joint
  angles on the pins in
  hma/machine/cylinders.py, and take no part in the simulation.
- The URDF was converted to MuJoCo MJCF. Joint origins, axes and the boom, arm and bucket
  limits are taken unchanged from zx120.urdf; the bucket tip site comes from bucket_end_joint.
- Not used: the URDF collision meshes and the Gazebo, ROS control and MoveIt files.
- Added: a free-floating base with twenty-four sphere-shaped track contact geoms, six along the
  inner and outer edge of each track at the bottom of the track mesh, a box geom carrying the
  counterweight mass in the lower 1 m of the rear of the upper structure, a site at the
  bucket tip, and two sites at the ends of the cutting edge, placed at the bucket mesh's
  widest points within 5 cm of the tip's distance from the bucket joint.
- Added at scene load, in hma/machine/model.py: convex collision shapes cut with planes from the
  track, body, boom, arm and bucket meshes (hma/machine/collision.py), with no mass and in a
  geom group that is not rendered by default, and a lifting point on the back of the bucket
  from which a block hangs by a four-leg sling joined at a master link. The cylinder and
  linkage meshes have no collision shape.
- Masses: the source gives a placeholder mass of 0.03 kg per link. The link masses used here are
  set in hma/machine/model.py. Boom (1,220 kg, with arm cylinder), arm (600 kg, with bucket
  cylinder), bucket (400 kg) and counterweight (2,450 kg) follow the manufacturer's published
  component weights; the split of the rest between undercarriage and upper structure is an
  estimate. Inertias are computed by MuJoCo from the convex hull of each mesh at uniform
  density, so centres of mass and inertias are estimates too.
- Joint force caps standing in for hydraulic relief limits are added in hma/machine/model.py;
  they are estimates derived from published digging forces and swing torque.

The conversion is reproducible with: python -I -m hma.machine.convert <path to zx120_ros>
